<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · Dance · 1918 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hava Nagila</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">הָבָה נָגִילָה</bdi> / <bdi lang="he-Latn">Hava Nagila</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A happy Hebrew song from 1918, made from an old Hasidic tune, that people sing and dance to at Jewish weddings and parties.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>disputed: Abraham Zevi Idelsohn or Moshe Nathanson (the text probably refined by Idelsohn)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (a 19th-century Hasidic niggun attributed to the Sadigurer Hasidim), arranged by Abraham Zevi Idelsohn or Moshe Nathanson</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Jews of Jerusalem and the Jewish diaspora</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jerusalem, Ottoman / British-occupied Palestine (present-day Israel / Palestine)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1918; first published and first recorded 1922</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words credited to Abraham Zvi Idelsohn (died 1938), first sung in 1918: in the public domain in the United States (published before 1930); where Moshe Nathanson is counted as their author they may still be protected)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The melody comes from a slow, 19th-century Hasidic niggun, a chant, attributed to the Sadigurer Hasidim, who lived in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Members of that community began to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1915. There Abraham Zevi Idelsohn, a professor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> University who was cataloguing all known Jewish music and teaching composition, gave the chant to his class and set them to add rhythm and words to make a modern Hebrew song. One of his students was the cantorial student Moshe Nathanson. Both men have been named as the song's author; the text was probably refined by Idelsohn, who wrote in 1932 that the melody had inspired him.</p><p>The song was made in 1918 to celebrate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration</a> and the British victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1917, and was first performed by a mixed choir at a concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Idelsohn printed his arrangement in 1922 in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> songbook Sefer Hashirim, and in the same year made its first commercial recording, for the Polyphon label, in a series of 39 Hebrew folk songs. The song spread quickly through the Jewish diaspora and became a fixture of weddings, bar and bat mitzvahs and other Jewish celebrations.</p><p>The version later known around the world was probably first recorded by the Israeli folk duo Ran &amp; Nama (Ran Eliran and Nechama Hendel) on their 1959 album. In the same year Harry Belafonte recorded it at Carnegie Hall; he rarely gave a concert without it and counted it, with &quot;The Banana Boat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, as one of the two songs that stood out in his career. From the late 1950s it was recorded in many styles: pop and orchestral versions, surf rock by Dick Dale and the Del Tones in 1963, a talking blues by Bob Dylan in the early 1960s, and a Cantonese adaptation by George Lam in 1987. John Lennon and Yoko Ono sang it during their Bed-In in 1969.</p><p>The song also crossed into sport. Supporters of AFC Ajax in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and of Tottenham Hotspur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, clubs whose fans have long used Jewish imagery, sing it in their stadiums. At the 2012 Summer Olympics the American gymnast Aly Raisman won the floor exercise final, the first American woman to take gold in that event, performing to its tune.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>As its title (&quot;Let us rejoice&quot;) says, it is a call to shared happiness. It tells no story: its few words invite the company to celebrate together, and the music quickens for dancing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הָבָה נָגִילָה וְנִשְׂמְחָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">Hava nagila ve-nismecha</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us rejoice and be glad,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">הָבָה נְרַנְּנָה</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">hava neranena</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us sing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="he" dir="rtl">עוּרוּ אַחִים בְּלֵב שָׂמֵחַ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="he-Latn">uru achim be-lev sameach.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>awake, brothers, with a joyful heart.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: The words as sung since 1918, credited to Abraham Zvi Idelsohn; as quoted in the Hebrew Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%94%D7%91%D7%94_%D7%A0%D7%92%D7%99%D7%9C%D7%94" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; in the public domain in the United States.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Modern Israeli Hebrew, as sung).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>The text gives each line once; in performance every line is repeated (&quot;Hava nagila, hava nagila, hava nagila ve-nismecha …&quot;), and &quot;uru achim&quot; is sung over and over before the close.</li><li>The first line echoes Psalm 118:24, &quot;This is the day which the Lord hath made; we will rejoice and be glad in it.&quot;</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="074_LF7GxQ8" data-title="Hava Nagila: Harry Belafonte, live at Carnegie Hall (1959)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Harry Belafonte, live at Carnegie Hall (1959)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Harry Belafonte - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=074_LF7GxQ8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Belafonte at Carnegie Hall (Harry Belafonte)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ran &amp; Nama – The First Record (Ran &amp; Nama)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dance Everyone Dance (Betty Madigan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1958</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Talkin' Hava Negeilah Blues (Bob Dylan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hava Nagila (Dick Dale and the Del Tones)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">surf rock recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Now! (Lena Horne)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1963</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Flying Circle (Frank Slay and his Orchestra)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1962</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">True Grit / Hava Nagila (Glen Campbell)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1969</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ecstasy (George Lam)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1987</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Cantonese adaptation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Jazz Singer (Neil Diamond)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Hava Song (Nissim Black)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Last Minute (Scooter)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">happy hardcore remix</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Niggun of the Sadigurer Hasidim (recreated by Daniel Gil from a song collected by Susman Kiselgof)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the melody</span></li></ul></section>
